Here's the maintainability analysis of your `InputValidatorTests`:

---

## Summary

Found **2 significant patterns** across 9 test methods in 1 file. The test suite is a classic candidate for parameterization — nearly every method follows the same 3-line structure with only the input and expected output changing.

---

## Finding 1: Copy-Paste Test Methods (High Priority)

**All 9 methods** share identical structure:
```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(<input>);
Assert.<bool>(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual(<msg>, result.ErrorMessage);
```

The **4 invalid-input tests** (`EmptyString`, `TooShort`, `ContainsSpecialChars`, `Null`, `WhitespaceOnly`) and **3 valid-input tests** (`ValidInput`, `MinLength`, `MaxLength`) can each collapse into a `[DataTestMethod]`:

**Before (5 separate methods for invalid cases):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_EmptyString_ReturnsFalse() { ... }
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_TooShort_ReturnsFalse() { ... }
// ...3 more identical methods
```

**After:**
```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("",    "Input cannot be empty",              DisplayName = "Empty string")]
[DataRow(null,  "Input cannot be empty",              DisplayName = "Null")]
[DataRow("   ", "Input cannot be empty",              DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
[DataRow("ab",  "Input must be at least 3 characters", DisplayName = "Too short")]
[DataRow("hello<script>", "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
public void Validate_InvalidInput_ReturnsFalse(string? input, string expectedError)
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate(input);
    Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
}
```

For valid inputs, the "max length" case uses `new string('a', 255)` which isn't a compile-time constant, so use `[DynamicData]`:

```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("hello-world", DisplayName = "Typical input")]
[DataRow("abc",         DisplayName = "Min length boundary")]
public void Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue(string input)
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate(input);
    Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
    Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
}

[TestMethod]
public void Validate_MaxLength_ReturnsTrue()
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate(new string('a', 255));
    Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
    Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
}
```

> **Impact:** 9 methods → 3 methods. Adding a new case = adding one `[DataRow]` line.

---

## Finding 2: Repeated Object Construction (Low Priority)

`new InputValidator()` appears in all 9 methods. You could extract it to `[TestInitialize]`:

```csharp
private InputValidator _validator = null!;

[TestInitialize]
public void Setup() => _validator = new InputValidator();
```

> **Trade-off:** This is a simple one-liner constructor, so the duplication is minor. It's worth doing alongside Finding 1, but wouldn't be worth it on its own.

---

## Refactoring Priority

| # | Finding | Impact | Risk |
|---|---------|--------|------|
| 1 | Parameterize with `[DataRow]` | High — eliminates 6 methods, makes new cases trivial | Low |
| 2 | Extract `InputValidator` construction | Minor — saves 1 line per method | Low |

The big win is **Finding 1** — it directly solves your pain of needing a whole new method for every test case. Want me to apply these refactorings?
